\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The binary icosahedral edge}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{2I} act on #{\mathbb C^2} as in \ref{fdip-0127}. The
stabilizer of #{e_1=(1,0)} is trivial, so the double cosets are
singletons. \ref{fdip-012D} gives a preserved space of rank
#{120}. For #{g\in 2I}, write the special unitary matrix of
\ref{fdip-007G} as
##{\begin{pmatrix}z&w\\-\overline w&\overline z\end{pmatrix}.}
Its first column is #{ge_1=(z,-\overline w)}. The product of
\ref{fdip-0127} gives
#{\langle e_1,ge_1\rangle=\overline z}, so
##{\operatorname{Re}\langle e_1,ge_1\rangle=\operatorname{Re}z
 =\frac12\trace g.}
The pairing kernel #{B(g)=2+\operatorname{Re}\langle e_1,ge_1\rangle}
is therefore constant on conjugacy classes. For a class-function
kernel, \ref{fdip-012L} says that the one-edge operator preserves
the class functions on #{2I}.}

\proof{
\p{The stabilizer and the order #{120} are \ref{fdip-0127}, so
\ref{fdip-012D} supplies one singleton double coset for each group
element. The displayed matrix form is \ref{fdip-007G}, so its
trace is #{z+\overline z} and its first column is the image of
#{(1,0)}. Conjugate linearity in the second slot of the product
sends that column to #{\overline z}. For square matrices of this
size, the trace of a product equals the trace of the reversed
product, because both expand to the same sum of entrywise
products. Hence
#{\trace(hgh^{-1})=\trace g}, and #{B(hgh^{-1})=B(g)}.
\ref{fdip-012L} applies.}
}
